===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|pronunciacioun}}, from {{der|en|frm|prononciation}}, {{m|frm|pronunciation}}, from {{der|en|la|prōnūntiātiō}}, noun of action from perfect passive participle {{m|la|prōnūntiātus}}, from verb {{m|la|prōnūntiāre||proclaim}}, from {{m|la|prō-||for}} + {{m|la|nūntiāre||announce}}. {{doublet|en|pronuntiatio}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|prə-nŭn'-sē-ā′-shən}}, {{IPA|en|/pɹəˌnʌn.siˈeɪ.ʃən/|[pʰɹəˌnʌn.siˈeɪ.ʃn̩]}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-pronunciation.ogg|a=US}}
* {{enPR|prə-noun'-sē-ā′-shən}} {{IPA|en|/pɹəˌnaʊn.siˈeɪ.ʃən/}} {{q|common but proscribed, corresponding to the misspelling {{m|en|pronounciation}}}}
* {{i|obsolete}} {{enPR|prə-nŭn'-shē-ā′-shən}}, {{IPA|en|/pɹəˌnʌn.ʃiˈeɪ.ʃən/|/pɹəˌnʌn.ʃiˈeɪ.ʃʌn/}}<ref>{{R:Jespersen MEGoHP|I|12.24|343}}</ref>
* {{hyph|en|pro|nun|ci|a|tion}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=5}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} The way in which a word, phrase, letter, character etc. is made to sound when spoken.
#: {{ux|en|What is the '''pronunciation''' of "hiccough"?}}
#: {{ux|en|In English, there are two '''pronunciations''' of the digraph 'th': voiced and unvoiced}}
#* {{RQ:Critical Pronouncing Dictionary|page=232|ppage=1|passage=☞ This word [{{m|en|earth}}] is liable to a coarſe vulgar '''pronunciation''', as if written ''Urth'';{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1957|year_published=1968|author=E[ric] J. Dobson|authorlink=Eric John Dobson|title= English pronunciation 1500-1700|edition=second|volume=II: Phonology|publisher=w:Clarendon Press|location=w:Oxford|oclc=1300760|page=673|section=§ 145|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/englishpronuncia0002dobs/page/673|passage=In spite of the vague evidence, I think that we may conclude that ME ''ǭ'', like ME ''ę̄'', had two '''pronunciations''' during the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, a more advanced pronunciation with close {{IPAchar|[oː]}}, and a more conservative one with the ‘intermediate vowel’ {{IPAchar|[ɔː]}} {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The characteristic sounds of a particular language, accent, or individual's speech.
#: {{ux|en|His Italian '''pronunciation''' is terrible.}}
#: {{ux|en|Being a good newsreader isn't just about having clear '''pronunciation'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Engliſh Teacher|first=Chriſtopher|last=Cooper|chapter=Chap. XIX: Of Barbarous Speaking|page=77-78|publisher2=Scolar Press|location2=Menston|publisher=''John Richardſon'', for the Author|location=London|year2=1969|year=1687|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/englishteacher1600coop/page/77/mode/1up|passage=He, that would write exactly, muſt avoid a Barbarous '''Pronunciation''', and conſider for facility, or thorow miſtake, many words are not ſounded after the beſt dialect. Such as {{...}} ''Wun'', one.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} The act of making a [[pronouncement]] or uttering a vocable.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1831|author=Thomas Oughton; James Thomas Law|title=Forms of Ecclesiastical Law|page=62
|passage=The second part is the sentence, which is the judge's '''pronunciation''' upon a cause depending between two in controversy.}}

